    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Live From The Los Angeles Sports Arena, April 26th, 1975\" captures Pink Floyd at the peak of their 1975 North American tour, a pivotal moment following the monumental success of The Dark Side of the Moon, which elevated them from British cult favorites to global arena rock titans. This meticulously restored and remastered release, drawn from a bootleg recording by the legendary Mike “Mike the Mic” Millard, presents the full 16-track set from their penultimate Los Angeles show at the Memorial Sports Arena, now available officially via Columbia/Sony Music in 2CD and 4LP formats. Clocking in at over two hours, it showcases the band's creative evolution, blending psychedelic rock with expansive soundscapes in a performance that bridges their past triumphs and future innovations.\n\nThe concert opens with proto-versions of Animals tracks—\"Raving and Drooling\" (later \"Sheep\") and \"You’ve Got To Be Crazy\" (later \"Dogs\")—demonstrating Pink Floyd workshopping new material live amid their transitional phase, before diving into Wish You Were Here previews like \"Shine On You Crazy Diamond\" (in two parts) bookending \"Have a Cigar.\" The set's core is a complete rendition of The Dark Side of the Moon, faithfully recreating its immersive suite of time, madness, and mortality themes with soaring guitars, atmospheric synths, and David Gilmour's haunting vocals, all enhanced by precise quadrophonic effects. It closes with the epic \"Echoes,\" underscoring their mastery of progressive rock's longue durée.\n\nSteven Wilson's restoration elevates Millard's surprisingly clear audience tape into the definitive document of Pink Floyd's 1975 sound—raw yet polished, immense in scale, and revered by collectors for its historical snapshot of a band dominating stadiums while forging ahead. Released for the first time on vinyl and CD, with a Record Store Day 4LP edition, this album highlights their enduring significance in rock history, offering fans unparalleled insight into the gestation of classics from Wish You Were Here and Animals amid rapturous reception.",
      "generated_at": "2026-04-22T18:28:35.358200",
      "model": "sonar",
      "source": "perplexity"
    }
